Date: Thu, 12 Aug 93 11:35:08 CDT
From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: investing

Investment Letter #2:

     PLOTTING INVESTMENTS ON THE RISK-RETURN GRAPH

Common stocks:

To find the average one year variability of a single stock, one need
only look at the annual high-low data in the newspaper.  Take a stock
that had a high of 40 and a low of 20 in the last 52 weeks (1 year).
The ratio is 2:1.  Take the sqaure root of the ratio, andf we get
1.41:1.  If we assume that a typical stock is midway in it's hig-low
range, we get an annual variability of 41%.  To gage the general risk
level of single stocks, sample a reasonably large number of stocks
off the stock page and find the average variability (I would guess
it to be around 30%).  

To find the risk level of a single specific stock, use microfilm
newpaper data or stock reporting services to get the year-to year
return of a stock.  Collect enough years for a sample (say at least
7 years) and find the average and standard deviation of of the
annual returns.

Mututal funds:

Get their prospectus and find the average annual return and caculate
the standard deviation of the annual return from the historical 
performance data in the prospectus.

Real Estate:

The return on real estate is composed of several parts: cash flow,
price increase from inflation,  equity buildup fropm mortgage payoff,
and tax benefits.  If you buy real estate with a small down payment,
then inflation is the dominant return factor.  In a given area, real
estate prices tend to go in multi-year cycles of low and high appreciation
rates.  Thus the variation in return will depend strongly on the
term you expect to hold the investment.  In other words, I consider
holding rental property for two years a different investment than
holding rental property for ten years.  In the latter case, the
return can be expected to be close to the overall inflation rate, and
the variability in the inflation rate is the major consideration.
In the former case, the risk is mostly determined by local conditions
of flat real estate prices or rapidly rising real estate prices and
the chance of the trend changing from one to the other.

Dani Eder
Meridian Investment Co.

