Date: Thu, 12 Aug 93 11:44:04 CDT
From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: how bad is the weight of Government regulation?

I disagree that the effect of more capital to invest is hard to translate
into a delta in the growth rate.  For new business investments, the
return on capital is something like 20-25% per year overall (obviously
there is a large spread, but this is an average number).  Thus if 25%
of the economy were re-invested and 20% return was achieved on that
capital, you would add 5% to the overall economic growth rate.  

To bound the estimates, one extreme is to assume that the money freed
up by slashing government would be invested in the same proportion
of consumption:capital investment as the rest of the economy today.
According to a US Govt. publication called "Economic Indicators", 
May 93, page 1, gross private domestic investment is 14% of GDP.  Then
adding 25% to the private economy would add 3.5% of GDP to investment, and
0.7 to 0.9% to GDP growth.  On the high end, if all the money went
to investment, the earlier figures would apply.

Dani Eder

