In academia it's the old "publish or perish" motivation that makes
me skeptical. I mentioned Harvard and Northwestern because they are
very prestigious biz schools(NW is # 1 last I checked). While I haven't
seen particular investment strategies reviewed I have seen ROI studies
based on fundamental analysis. If a professor had a thesis that he
wished to advance and his numbers didn't jive(support his thesis) he
might be tempted to "fudge" results. The danger of being called on the
work is far less than that of someone in the physical sciences lets
say. The reward is the list-o-pubs gets longer.

Last year an investment newsletter published the findings that 
switching to last years winner in mutual funds was a sucessful
strategy using a ten year sample I believe. However my own
un-scientific investigations with one particular fund family were
quite the contrary. If you switched into Fidelity Select Biotech
the year after it's best year you would have lost your shirt.
The fund still is in the top 10 within the family for a ten
year period but that one year loss(and indeed the funds two year
performance) is above most small investor's threshold of pain.

For newsletters we have the Hulbert Guide to track performance.
I know of no such publication for academics.

caveat emptor.

-sjm

