Is Friedman claiming this is pairwise efficient? I understood it to depend
mostly on externalities, that the gain experienced by thrid parties was
greater than the loss of the principals.

I found the post strange, but didn't have time to really examine it. It
examines thing primarily from the standpoint of the author, but our
publishing institutions also depend on intellectual property. How would the
publishing industry change if there were no intellectual property?

Is there any historical data on this question, any reasonably advanced
society that had/has institutions for distributing intellectual goods
without a property basis? I've heard that there are countries now that
don't recognize copyrights from other countries (or don't enforce
intellectual property rights robustly). Communist countries have
alternative intellectual property in that the state controls publishing and
frowns on wildcat publishers. There was a certain amount of pirated western
literature in samizdat. But these are hardly the robust publishing
institutions responding adequitely to market demand I would hope for. Is
Friedman saying that the publishing industry is a mistake?

Further, even if the efficiency argument goes through, how does one
prohibit contracts under anarchocapitalism that approximate intellectual
property? Maybe it's (socially) inefficient to enforce them, but does that
stop anyone from trying?
