From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: VC investment strategies

In my "daydream investments" post I mentioned buying Autocad instead
of just doing my own startup.  Here's the rationale: Autocad has 
an up-and-running software distribution & user help system for CAD 
users.  I want to introduce new kinds of software, not reinvent 
distribution and servicing.  (put another way, I don't want to add 
another layer of risk).  Same reason Symantec bought Norton, I think,
to consolidate distribution, marketing & servicing.

The actual software development might be done outside the Autocad 
bureaucracy by a separately funded startup, if necessary.

I see lots of opportunity in cypherpunk-related businesses:
digital cash, e-banks, offshore markets, offshore VC funds,
algorithmic auditing services, etc.   Important is that these
are service businesses, not attempts to sell software.  Good
startups will combine trade-secreted software with related 
traditional services (eg traditional offshore banking done
electronically/protected cryptographically) and employees
with extensive experience in that service market.

The difference between investment and charity is a fine but important
line.  There's nothing wrong with charity as long as it's clearly 
labelled.  A big problem is that promoters like Drexler keep mixing up 
charity and investement.  Drexler-style nanotechnology is charity
(except for that small part of cell-repair machine design that might
be funded out of the cryonics advertising budget, if cryonics becomes
big).  Near-term biotech, designer catalyists, submicron litho, etc. 
are in contrast potentially profitable investments, because the
tools and knowledge we have about them are already extensive, it
takes only a small increment of research to bring them to market.

Imagine a search space of future business opportunities, which Tim May
calls "k-space".  This space is filled with deserts, oases,
fertile valleys, veins of gold, dry holes, etc. (In this space
we also freely mix metaphors. :-)  In this world
companies are a bunch of cowardly penguins, each sitting around on 
the edge of the ice.  In the water are lucrative schools of fish, waiting
to be eaten, and also seals that might eat the first penguins to
jump in.  So, other penguins wait for somebody else to jump in
and take the risk, then jump in themselves.  (But conditions might
vary.  In some cases the school of fish will be close and the
seals far away, and it pays to jump in first.  But in most cases
the location of the fish and seals are highly uncertain, we just
know they're out there somewhere, swimming around).

So here are the penguins, each waiting another penguin to jump in
first (taking the risks of getting eaten by a seal), then the IBMs of 
the world jump in later and grab most of the wealth.  IBM
waited too long too many times when the fish were
close and the seals far away, but in many other cases the "coward" 
strategy worked.  

For charity to be most effective, we should concentrate
it at the point where it pushes the first penguin over the edge.  
To follow up, a profitable investment would then protect that penguin 
from the seals.  Since we've already started mapping the water, pointing 
out seals to avoid, fish schools to eat, etc. we can better anticipate 
the fortunes of penguins as they jump in.  

With the exception of cryonics (which needs to learn about and
communicate far-future theoretical applied science, eg Drexler/Foresight
Institute) that charity should be concentrated at the "bleeding edge" 
of k-space, where the VCs and commercial research labs are just
starting to get interested in, and we the investors with some
foresight see large veins of gold just beyond.  Once the vein
of gold becomes clearly reachable via affordable trade-secretable 
R&D is the time to start up the for-profit company.

(Hah!  Now I've beaten Tim May's record for mixed metaphor!)

The drug business is quite profitable, even though Clinton might
do severe harm. A few patents or trade secrets costing
a few $100,000 of research can turn it billions.  (Or could
be lost, but the average profit is good).  With good 
crypto-anarchy and/or a more open-minded government things
like smart drugs, cryonics, neural transplants, etc. could
take off.  Each of those are potentially $100 billion/year
markets that can be cracked open with a few $million of
research (and most of that is for the stupid FDA tests, 
not research necessary to actually design useful products
or do tests that would provide reasonable confidence in
a crypto-anarchic society).

(Another use for auditing protocol: verification of the
data obtained from scientific tests.  Fraud is a very
big issue in biotech research, cf. big controversies
over NIH fraud cases played out on the pages of _Science_, etc.)

We can't invest retrospectively.  VC is high risk, but 
I think investors with a broader and longer-range view of
society, combined with major knowledge of the industry (and
I would certainly do extensive homework before plunking
down any money!), such investors will win more often than
William Oldmoney III who inherited his $millions and his broker 
says XYZ looks pretty hot today. ("Think plastics, son!").

VCs have to figure in a nearly 40% tax hit for converting capital gains in
stock to cash.  Sounds like an opportunity for crypto-offshore VC to me, 
if some sort of auditing protocol can be set up.  (Since it's a new venture
it would have no track record/reputation for customers to rely on).

We should learn from the failure of Xanadu, Amix, etc.
>From an outsider's perspective, I percieved Xanadu as more a 
movement than a company.  But I've always been dubious about hypertext.  
I was wrong, but still closer to the truth than all the hype surrounding 
it.  I bet that most hypertext ventures have lost money -- even though the 
concept is now turning out to be useful (eg on-line software manuals, 
CD-ROM) it's not as useful as was originally hyped.  Also, it's just one 
of many software techniques, it can't be (or at least wasn't) made
trade secret or patented, so it's not really something I'd build
a business around.   

The big gulf between ideology-driven dreaming and implementation
is part of the underlying rationale to separate the large going
end-venture like Autocad from the small new ideas with which to
seed Autocad with new products.  It would be silly to spend $billions or 
even more than $1 million in most cases on these ideas.  Better to
find a path that grows a few $100,000 of R&D at a time,
with some of that R&D being a charity penguin-nudge and the
rest invested in ready-to-implement trade secrets & the like
which provide a return on the R&D.  Of course the actual $amounts
here vary -- if I indeed had $100 billion to throw around they
would be much bigger than they are in real life!

